  "query_preview": "..After making landfall earlier this evening, the \nremnants of Tropical Storm Rachel are moving towards the north-\nnortheast early this morning before exiting into Utah this \nafternoon. Associated showers and thunderstorms continue to move \nquickly northward through the region, remaining fairly scattered \nacross our area. With a continued strong southerly flow, moisture \nhas continued to advect into the region and have left ripe \nconditions with the potential for heavy rain through today. Models \ncontinue to highlight areas across central/western Arizona as being \nat the greatest risk for flash flooding. Model soundings show PWATs \nthat are near or above the daily max. QPF amounts will vary widely \nas we see more of a convective element to the precipitation vs a \nwidespread stratiform event. Rainfall amounts between 1-3\" still \nseem reasonable with locally higher amounts possible. The Flood Watch \nremains in effect for areas along and south of the Mogollon Rim up \nthrough the Kaibab Plateau.\n\nStorms should be moving pretty quickly as current RAP 0-6 km winds \nare around 40-50 kts. The speed of the storms may help to limit some \nof the flash flood potential, though some training of storms may \noccur. As far as a severe component goes, there is a small amount of \ninstability that may increase slightly today if the sun is able to \npeek out for a bit. Shear is deep and strong though appears to be \nlargely unidirectional through the low and mid levels on model \nsoundings. There will be potential for some strong storms to develop \nwith mainly a damaging wind threat with the threat for a tornado \nremaining pretty low but not zero.  \n\nIncreasing south to southwest will occur through the day today as \nthe gradient remains tight and upper jet moves overhead. For today, \nwinds will be south to southwest 25-35 mph with gusts 40-50 mph and \nsome locally higher gusts possible. Winds remain strong on Monday \nand Tuesday, mainly across the higher terrain but really begin to \ntaper off later Tuesday. A Wind Advisory remains in effect through \nthis evening. \n\nThe remnant circulation pushes off to the north as a trough deepens \nacross the western states. Dry air gets pulled into the mid-levels \nalong the eastern side of the trough and helps to dry us out as we \nhead towards mid-week. However, with moisture remaining in the low \nlevels, expect scattered showers and thunderstorms to continue \nthrough at least Tuesday before ramping down in coverage Tuesday \nafternoon/evening. By Wednesday, conditions start to dry out and are \nexpected to continue into Thursday. \n\nBeyond Wednesday, there remains uncertainty as to how the pattern \nwill evolve through the rest of the week. The trough looks to become \ncut off from the main flow, lingering near the Four Corners region \nthrough much of the week. Models continue to disagree on how fast \nthis low will move to the east which leaves us with low confidence \nfor the extended forecast.",
